// group borrowing
// https://verdagon.dev/blog/group-borrowing

// about invalidating child groups when data (pointers, enums) are changed
xs: [..]i4= []
a : >i4 = xs.add(1)
xs.add(2)
// a is now invalid
// why... because xs.data may have been realloc'ed to increase the capacity for .add(2)
// so when xs.data= realloc(xs.data', new_size)
//  all elements in xs.data's subgroup should become invalid
// this is >xs.data[0], >xs.data[1], ..., etc, >xs.data[n]

// so what is a group
// a group is something that can be destroyed independently
// xs is
// {
//    data: >'i4
//    pos: n4
// }
// the first group is xs, xs.data, and xs.pos
//  as none of these can be destroyed independently, they are all values
// the second group is xs.data[n], all of these elements can be destroyed
//  by e.g. realloc, free
// xs.data owns group two, it's modification invalidates group two,
//  the modification of pos does not
//                   xs
//      data                    pos
//  data[0] data[1]

xs: [*]i4

x1:= xs.add(1)
x2:= xs.add(2)
// x1 is still valid, and will be as long as xs lives
//  because it's a dynamic link pointer, the memory is not realloc'ed it's added to a list

MyData :: data {...}

Arena :: data(T: Type) {
  total_cap: i4
  entrys: [..]ArenaEntry(T)
}

ArenaEntry :: data(T: Type) {
  pos: i4
  data: >'T
}

a: Arena(MyData)= {}

// g1: a, a.total_cap, a.entrys, a.entrys.data, a.entrys.pos
// g2: a.entrys[n], a.entrys[n].pos, a.entrys[n].data
// g3: a.entrys[n].data[m]

x:= a.add({...}) // x is >a.entrys[0].data[0], so group 3
a.add({...})
invalidates g2, but not g3?, so x is still valid
